{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793017807,"nonce":"6B/swMd2dydfXSCLowBU8TehA40=","title":"Plan Ashui UI studio for inspection and visual composition","message":"Status: planning only, explicitly requested by the user. Implementation is not started.\n\nProduct scope\n- Add an Ashui UI studio in RayDE: a side panel for inspection and a tab view for visual UI composition.\n- A Haxe file containing a class extending ashui.ui.Component\u003cT\u003e can switch between Code and Studio preview while retaining the same document and unsaved edits.\n- Studio preview has Edit mode for selecting, inspecting and composing UI, and interactive Presentation mode for exercising the component's behaviour.\n- Projects without a .ashuiproj file open component files as code only. Offer an explicit Open in Studio action in the context menu for eligible files.\n- A .ashuiproj project enables the studio workflow for eligible components. The initial view in those projects and persistence of per-file opt-in choices remain product decisions.\n\nExisting integration points inspected\n- ashui/canvaskit/haxe/ashui/canvaskit/CanvasKit.hx: CanvasKitProps exposes draw/paint callbacks, interactive input, a Viewport2D, selection, click/hover/drag callbacks and region registration. CanvasKit already supplies pan, zoom, selection and hit regions.\n- ashui/haxe/ashui/layout/LayoutTree.hx: layout computation and node hit testing.\n- ashui/haxe/ashui/layout/DisplayList.hx: update(tree, root, scale) builds ordered rendering records and exposes their byte buffer and record count. The format includes text, images, clipping, layers and embedded canvases.\n- ashui/haxe/ashui/ui/Component.hx: typed props, optional children/tree, render ownership and cleanup; components expose their rendered element rather than adding a wrapper box.\n- RayDE/src/rayde/ui/Workbench.hx and RayDE/src/rayde/workspace/PageHost.hx: investigate document/tab and preview-host integration.\n\nPlanned work\n1. Rendering feasibility: establish how to mount a real component, obtain its LayoutTree/root, compute layout and display its DisplayList inside Ashui's CanvasKit. Evaluate reusing the existing renderer through the paint hook versus compositing a preview render target. Define coordinate transforms, scale, clips, text/image resources, layers and nested canvases. The existing canvas callbacks do not establish a ready-made tree/display-list loader; demonstrate the bridge first.\n2. Component and project discovery: resolve the Component\u003cT\u003e superclass identity, including aliases/inheritance as supported, identify candidate classes in a file and choose the preview entry. Define .ashuiproj discovery/schema for entries, fixtures/default props, viewport, theme/assets and the configured project build. Resolve required props, children and runtime-service fixtures. Reuse project target/defines and macro configuration.\n3. Preview lifecycle: choose the preview runtime/host and the transport/ownership contract for tree inspection data, display-list records and renderer resources. Handle unsaved source versions, asynchronous rebuilds, cancellation, diagnostics and replacement of old previews. Dispose component owners, subscriptions, timers and GPU resources when a preview is replaced or closed.\n4. Studio shell and inspection: add the Code/Studio switch, canvas view and inspection side panel. Link canvas selection to a component/node hierarchy and typed props/layout/style inspection. Keep source locations available for Reveal in Code. Keep editor selection/manipulation gestures distinct from Presentation mode's component pointer, focus and keyboard interaction.\n5. Visual composition and source round trips: define an initial editable set of props/layout/style changes and child insertion/reordering. Map runtime nodes to Haxe/markup source ranges and apply document edits with undo/redo while preserving surrounding source. Define how dynamic or generated nodes participate in inspection and which have editable source mappings. Haxe source remains the authoritative component definition.\n6. Project gating: eligible files in projects without .ashuiproj stay in code view until the context-menu opt-in. Reuse the open document when switching views. Define loading/error/unsupported states and the policy for restoring the last successful preview while a new version builds.\n\nAcceptance checks for later implementation\n- Code-only projects open eligible and ordinary Haxe files normally; Open in Studio is offered for eligible components and activates the alternate view only when selected.\n- Studio projects expose Code/Studio switching for eligible files; selection and unsaved edits survive switches.\n- Edit and Presentation modes have the intended selection, focus and input behaviour.\n- A mounted component renders its real layout/display list in the canvas, including nested components, text/images, transforms and clipping.\n- Inspector selection reveals the correct source; supported visual edits update that document and participate in undo/redo.\n- Preview build failures, rapid edits and switching tabs remain responsive; obsolete preview results are discarded.\n- Repeated opening, replacing and closing previews releases runtime and renderer resources.\n\nOpen design decisions\n- .ashuiproj schema and default view when it is present.\n- Preview compiler/runtime, isolation and cross-host renderer resource transport.\n- Fixture conventions for required props and services, multiple component classes per file, and the initial source-editing coverage.","files":null}]}